Instrumentation used to find the cause of non-deterministic replay. Not part of the build.

Apply from the repository root with:

    git apply local-loop/patches/hash-probe.patch

and run the engine with -Doba.hashprobe=/tmp/probe.txt. It writes two files per vehicle:
<path>.<vehicleId> and <path>.entries.<vehicleId>.

WHAT IT MEASURED, AND WHY EACH PART EXISTS
------------------------------------------
Provenance. Each dump names the jar every patched class was loaded from, via
getProtectionDomain().getCodeSource(). A class shadowed on the classpath that loses to another jar
produces no error and no warning, so without this line a negative result cannot be distinguished from
"the patch was never loaded". One earlier conclusion was wrong for exactly this reason.

Hash values vs iteration order are DIFFERENT questions. The dump has two sections. The sorted section
answers "is the hash of a given content key the same in two JVMs". The unsorted ITERATION ORDER
section answers "does the container yield its elements in the same sequence". A probe that sorts its
output cannot answer the second, and conflating them cost a run.

Once per vehicle, not once per JVM. Each vehicle's first record is a separate question. A JVM-wide
one-shot probe measures whichever vehicle happens to arrive first and says nothing about the others.

n / orderChecksum / sampled, in the .entries dump, separate three failures that look alike:
  n differs                      -> the distribution has a different number of entries
  orderChecksum differs, n same  -> same entries, different sequence, OR the entry hashes moved
                                    (the checksum folds in hashCode, so it cannot separate these two;
                                    to distinguish them, dump value-based content keys per position)
  both same, sampled differs     -> same vector and same draw, so look at handleSort/_objIdx

Candidate sets legitimately contain null. See ParticleFactoryImpl's own null guard, and
MotionModelImpl's transitions.add(newParentBlockStateObs). An unguarded probe throws out of
createParticles, which both loses the measurement and perturbs the run.

A probe must never change what it measures. The body is wrapped so a RuntimeException is logged
rather than propagated.

WHAT IT FOUND
-------------
Three identity-hash holes reaching Particle, and therefore reaching the iteration order of every
hash-ordered container on the inference path:
  BlockConfigurationEntryImpl  - declares no hashCode      (upstream, shadowed)
  TripEntryImpl                - declares no hashCode      (upstream, shadowed)
  java.lang.Enum.hashCode()    - final, identity-based, folded in at VehicleState and JourneyState

The enum was the last one and the least visible: Enum declares a hashCode, so a scan for "classes
lacking hashCode" does not find it. It cannot be overridden, so the fix is at the call sites that fold
it in, which means the audit surface is "places that fold a hash", not "classes lacking one".

